Z||000000||88GT||01-14-2000||12:13 AM||||I have been looking for an 88GT for a reasonable price for a little while now.  Today I picked up a local auto trader and there it was.  1988 GT, Red, new tires.  Only  $1995.00.  So I called and it was already sold.  This car had 160,000 miles and would have been just what I wanted.  I have my wife's OK to do a body conversion and the other things that go along with that (engine swap, suspension upgrade, etc. :-))  Now I'll just have to wait {:-(||||reg||9
Z||000001||william_thorniii||01-14-2000||12:23 AM||||88GT,<P>Why are you looking for an 88GT when you want to change the body, engine and suspension?  If you do not like the GT "flying butress" style body, save your $ and buy a coupe.  If you do not want the 2.8L engine see my previous sentence.  The famed 88 only truly differed from other years in that its suspension was massaged.  There were minor other asthetic differences as well.  My question however stands... why are you looking for an 88 GT?  It is the most expensive model and all you intend on using is the interior, chassis and transmission?  I am not flaming you, just don't understand.  You could have a Fiero now.  Go to the boneyard, find someone's with a blown engine.  Save your $ on the initial purchase and spend it on your street screamer engine.<P>Of course this is all just my insignificant opinion.<P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||5
